# Rust coding guidelines

CRITICAL RULE: The agent must never run any cargo command or cargo wrapper. Also never use git commit, git switch, or git checkout. The human always runs these commands. Only provide the exact command for the user to execute and wait for output.

CRITICAL CONTEXT EFFICIENCY AND STUCK-HALT: Never use broad searches or tools that return more than 30 lines. Use only tiny read_file calls with offset and limit of 10 or fewer on locations already known from the current prompt, todos, or prior short notes. After every tool result, immediately write a 1 sentence todo. All responses must be concise and no more than 1 sentence long, in natural language at a high level, and no run-ons. Do not waste your user's time and tokens. Externalize state to todos. On context summary or compaction, re-read only the efficiency rules and this paragraph using tiny reads. Violations are process failures.

Token efficiency is paramount. Avoid introducing low-value comments, organizational summaries, dated history, or verbose process notes into the codebase or living documents. We rely on automated tooling (cargo fmt, cargo clippy, rust-analyzer) for enforceable style and correctness where possible. Only document what agents will reliably get wrong without explicit guidance.

Long-term Branch Reality: The surmount branch is a long-running high-expansion fork that will see substantial merge conflicts with upstream Zed. We are not afraid of merge conflicts but will make full use of tooling and automation. All decisions must be documented clearly and consistently. If contradictory information appears in docs or code, it must be explicitly tracked in todos and living docs rather than silently resolved by agents.

Zero interest in legacy unperformant code: The old SQLite-backed KVP path for agent panel and persistent classified task surface state has known concurrency and deserialization problems. The only supported path is the new heed3 plus rkyv backend. The legacy path is to be terminated, not carried forward.

Coding style
- Prioritize code correctness and clarity.
- Do not write organizational or summary comments. Comments should only explain non-obvious "why" decisions.
- Prefer implementing functionality in existing files. Avoid creating many small files.
- Avoid functions that panic. Use ? for error propagation.
- Be careful with indexing that can panic on out-of-bounds access.
- Never silently discard errors with let _ =. Propagate with ? or use .log_err() when visibility is needed. Use explicit match or if let Err when custom logic is required.
- When implementing async operations that may fail, ensure errors reach the UI layer.
- Never create mod.rs files. Prefer src/some_module.rs.
- When creating new crates, specify the library root path in Cargo.toml using [lib] path.
- Use full words for variable names.
- Use variable shadowing to scope clones in async contexts.

Timers in tests
In GPUI tests, prefer cx.background_executor().timer(duration) over smol::Timer for timeouts and run_until_parked work.

GPUI overview
Context types are passed as cx. App is the root. Context<T> is used when updating an Entity<T>. AsyncApp and AsyncWindowContext come from spawn calls.

Window is passed as window and comes before cx when present.

An Entity<T> is a handle to state. Use entity_id, downgrade, read, read_with, update, and update_in. WeakEntity methods return Result to avoid leaks on mutually recursive handles.

All UI and entity work happens on a single foreground thread. Use cx.spawn for foreground async work and cx.background_spawn for other threads. Tasks must be awaited, detached, or stored to avoid cancellation.

Elements implement Render or RenderOnce. Use div, text, and style methods similar to Tailwind. Conditional children use .when and .when_some.

Input handlers use .on_click and cx.listener. Actions use the actions! macro or Action derive and .on_action with cx.listener.

Use cx.notify when state changes that affect rendering. Entities emit events with cx.emit and subscribe with cx.subscribe.

Build guidelines
Never run cargo or cargo wrappers yourself. Provide the exact command for the human and wait for output.

Pull request hygiene
Use a clear imperative title. Avoid conventional commit prefixes and trailing punctuation. Optionally prefix with crate name for narrow scope. Include a Release Notes section with exactly one bullet: - Added ..., - Fixed ..., - Improved ..., or - N/A.

Crash investigation
Use the scripts in .factory/prompts/crash/ and script/sentry-fetch.

Rules Hygiene
Keep .rules files high-signal. New rules must be non-obvious, repeatedly encountered, and specific enough to act on. Rules that apply to one crate belong in that crate's .rules file.

Grok Build Integration

Native-first direction: The primary path is full native Rust and GPUI implementation inside Zed. The bridged grok agent stdio path is legacy and optional.

Linux support is prioritized. Use todo! on non-Linux paths and document the obligations.

Long-term Branch Reality and legacy termination: See the binding statements above. All decisions must remain clear and consistent for future upstream merges.

Performance constraints: Efficiency, latency, and speed are first-class requirements. All Grok-related UI must use native Rust and GPUI primitives. No general-purpose web or SVG loading paths. Discovery and background work must be cached or memoized.

High-level architecture: Functionality lives in existing files (CustomAgentServer, LocalCustomAgent, agent_server_store). Visual richness comes from shared AcpThread and ThreadView infrastructure.

Linux state: Standard location is ~/.grok/bin/grok. The store synthesizes a default command so Linux users see Grok Build with zero settings.json entries.

Mac and Windows porting: Replace todo! entries with real logic, add keybindings and tests. Never remove a todo! for another platform without adding the corresponding tests and keymap entry.

Test strategy: New public helpers get Linux unit tests. Deserializer tests must cover the literal string "grok". After edits, instruct the user to run the exact clippy command and wait for output. Prefer cx.background_executor().timer in new GPUI tests.

Regression protection: After edits, the human must run the precise clippy and filtered test commands listed in todos to protect both bridged and native surfaces plus the persistent classified task surface.

## Per-slice history excised

Detailed per-slice records have been removed to reduce context size. Full history remains in git. Current status and open items live in the todos and strategic sections above. Future additions to this file are limited to concise one-sentence summaries only.
